CloudBurrow patch 102 to github.com/goccy/bigquery-emulator v0.8.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1823).

The bug: BigQuery runs jobs side by side. The emulator answered one
request at a time (sequentialAccessMiddleware), so while a slow statement
ran, every other request waited for its end: another client's query, a
jobs.list and an INFORMATION_SCHEMA.JOBS query among them. A query sent
during a 20 s query reached its 10 s timeoutMs, a jobs.list answered only
when the slow query ended, and a running job could not be seen RUNNING.

The patch:
- opens the temporary database in WAL mode and without a shared cache, so
  that a reader reads a snapshot of the last commit and neither waits for
  the writer nor holds it up (a shared cache's readers and writers of one
  table fail rather than wait);
- lets a request that only reads run without the write lock: the GET
  methods, and a jobs.query or query jobs.insert of one SELECT statement
  in GoogleSQL (selectStatement reads the statement's tokens; DML, DDL,
  scripts, legacy SQL, a compressed body and anything unclear take the
  lock). Such a query's statement runs in a transaction that only reads;
  its job, and a jobs.insert's destination table, are then written under
  the write lock, in a transaction of their own;
- keeps every other request under the write lock for its whole length, as
  before, and begins its transactions IMMEDIATE, so that no read beside
  it makes it begin from a snapshot it can no longer write over;
- keeps a read request off the connections of other requests' open
  transactions (the reads of a lazy dataset's tables used the newest
  one's), and a read request's transactions out of that list: it reads on
  a connection of its own.
An in-memory database (MemoryStorage) and a --database file keep the one
request lock.

